{
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Case Sixteen.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.881,
   "pause": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "The Lighthouse Path.",
   "start": 2.481,
   "end": 3.718,
   "pause": 1.4
  },
  {
   "text": "The old lighthouse on the point is a little museum now, and Hedley Truscott is its keeper. In the lamp room at the top stands a polished brass telescope that once belonged to the very first lighthouse keeper.",
   "start": 5.118,
   "end": 18.067,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "On Tuesday at two o'clock exactly, Hedley climbed out onto the gallery to clean the windows. At a quarter past two he came back in. In those fifteen minutes, the telescope disappeared.",
   "start": 18.917,
   "end": 30.523,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Three people had been seen around the village that afternoon, and each of them had been seen twice.",
   "start": 31.373,
   "end": 36.685,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Wenna Polglaze was seen in the Kettle and Gull at two o'clock, and again at twenty past two.",
   "start": 37.535,
   "end": 42.932,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Opie, a visitor with binoculars always around his neck, was seen at the harbour at a quarter to two, and again at half past two.",
   "start": 43.782,
   "end": 52.337,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Pip Carew was seen in the bookshop at five to two, and again at ten past two.",
   "start": 53.187,
   "end": 57.603,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The paths up to the point are steep and rocky. Everybody in Tidewhistle Cove agrees on these times, and agrees that nobody could possibly do the journeys any faster. From the Kettle and Gull to the lighthouse is fifteen minutes. From the harbour to the lighthouse is twenty minutes. From the bookshop to the lighthouse is ten minutes. Each journey takes exactly as long going back down as it does going up.",
   "start": 58.453,
   "end": 82.858,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Agnes Bell took out a pencil and an old receipt, then put the pencil down and decided to do it in her head instead.",
   "start": 83.708,
   "end": 90.961,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Here is the puzzle again. The telescope vanished between two o'clock and a quarter past two. Wenna was at the tea room at two and at twenty past, fifteen minutes from the lighthouse. Mr Opie was at the harbour at a quarter to two and at half past, twenty minutes from the lighthouse. Pip was at the bookshop at five to two and at ten past, ten minutes from the lighthouse.",
   "start": 91.811,
   "end": 113.913,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Can you solve it? Who is the only person who could have reached the lighthouse in time to take the telescope?",
   "start": 115.013,
   "end": 120.922,
   "pause": 1.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Think about it.",
   "start": 122.122,
   "end": 123.146,
   "pause": 0.9
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
   "start": 124.046,
   "end": 129.273,
   "pause": 4.5
  },
  {
   "text": "The Solution.",
   "start": 133.773,
   "end": 134.797,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Opie took the telescope.",
   "start": 135.897,
   "end": 137.817,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Take Wenna first. She was at the tea room at two o'clock, so the earliest she could reach the lighthouse was a quarter past two, just as Hedley came back in. And to be back at the tea room by twenty past two, she would have had to leave the lighthouse by five past two. She can't arrive at a quarter past and leave at five past, so it wasn't Wenna.",
   "start": 138.667,
   "end": 159.061,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Now Pip. He was at the bookshop at five to two, so the earliest he could reach the lighthouse was five past two. But to be back in the bookshop by ten past two, he would have had to leave the lighthouse by two o'clock. He can't arrive at five past and leave at two, so it wasn't Pip.",
   "start": 159.911,
   "end": 176.978,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Now Mr Opie. He left the harbour at a quarter to two at the earliest, so he could have reached the lighthouse by five past two. To be back at the harbour by half past two, he only needed to leave the lighthouse by ten past two. That gives him five minutes in the lamp room while Hedley was out on the gallery. He is the only one who could have done it.",
   "start": 177.828,
   "end": 198.308,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Opie was a keen collector of anything to do with lighthouses, and he admitted that the telescope had been too much for him. He returned it, and Hedley now lets him look through it, under supervision, every Tuesday at two.",
   "start": 199.158,
   "end": 212.726,
   "pause": 0.0
  }
 ],
